==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fr}} {{term|lang=fr|bigot||a bigot, hypocrite}}, from {{etyl|frm}} {{term|bigot|lang=frm}}, from {{etyl|fro}} {{term|lang=fro|bigot}}, originally a derogatory term applied to Normans for their frequent note of the {{etyl|ang}} oath {{term||bī god|[[by God]]|lang=ang}}. It is not know, however, whether the precise Germanic language of origin is English: compare {{etyl|gmh|-}} {{term||bī got|lang=gmh}}, {{etyl|dum|-}} {{term||bi gode|lang=dum}}.

An alternate etymology (Liberman, Grammont, et al.) derives the Old French word from {{term|Albigot||[[Albigensian]] heretic}} [http://blog.oup.com/2011/10/bigot-2/].

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈbɪɡət/}}, {{X-SAMPA|/"bIg@t/}}, {{enPR|bĭgʹət}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# One who is obstinately or [[intolerant]]ly devoted to his or her own opinions and [[prejudice]]s.
# One who is strongly [[partial]] to one's own [[group]], [[religion]], [[race]], or [[politics]] and is [[intolerant]] of those who differ.

